talk(572,'1982-10-18','Jürgen Fröhlich','P001054-P001059','Random lattice surfaces, roughening, and the proposed Regge continuum limit',r'The mathematical entry occupies P001055--P001057. The first model is an integer-valued solid-on-solid height graph with a rigorous roughening result; the later questions concern more general plaquette surfaces and their topology. A closely matching 1982 primary lecture text makes this distinction explicit. Boundary-independent exponential growth was developed in a 1983 paper by the speaker and collaborators, but neither a power-law correction nor a general continuum-limit theorem follows from it. The final Regge curvature theorem is kept separate from the proposed convergence of random-metric integrals.')
add(572,'Four motivations for statistical mechanics of surfaces','P001055','remark',r'Applications listed are crystal growth, phase interfaces, lattice gauge theories and dual-resonance strings.',r'These motivate different ensembles: height graphs approximate interfaces, gauge expansions produce plaquette configurations, and string functional integrals sum over maps and metrics. Sharing a surface interpretation does not identify their measures or critical exponents.',r'The same four motivations appear in \citet[Introduction]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces}.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces',),literal='correct',verification='read the result',match='exact')
add(572,'The solid-on-solid measure with zero boundary heights','P001055-P001056','definition',r'For finite \(\Lambda\subset\mathbb Z^2\), \(h_p\in\mathbb Z\), \(h_p=0\) outside, add vertical walls and use \(e^{-\beta\operatorname{area}(S)}=e^{-\beta|\Lambda|}\prod_{\langle p,q\rangle}e^{-\beta|h_p-h_q|}\).',r'Let \(\mathcal H_\Lambda=\{h:\mathbb Z^2\to\mathbb Z:h=0\text{ outside }\Lambda\}\). Sum each unordered nearest-neighbor edge meeting \(\Lambda\) once and set \(H_\Lambda(h)=\sum_{\langle p,q\rangle}|h_p-h_q|\). For \(\beta>0\), \(Z_\Lambda=\sum_h e^{-\beta H_\Lambda(h)}<\infty\), and \(\mu_{\Lambda,\beta}=Z_\Lambda^{-1}e^{-\beta H_\Lambda}\). The constant horizontal area cancels in normalization; this model excludes overhangs.',r'Compare \citet[Section~7, equation~(7.16)]{frohlichspencer1981transition}.',('frohlichspencer1981transition',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The bond-counting convention and finite-volume state space are explicit.')
add(572,'Localization and exponential height tails at large inverse temperature','P001056','theorem',r'For large \(\beta\), \(\mathbb E h_p^2\) is bounded and \(\Pr(|h_p|=n)\sim e^{-{\rm const}\,n}\), attributed to Dobrushin.',r'For sufficiently large \(\beta\), zero-boundary SOS measures satisfy a volume-uniform bound \(\Pr(|h_p|\ge n)\le C_\beta e^{-c_\beta n}\), hence \(\sup_{\Lambda,p}\mathbb E h_p^2<\infty\). A Peierls contour argument proves such exponential bounds; the displayed \(\sim\) is not by itself a verified sharp tail asymptotic.',r'\citet[Section~7, Remark~2 after Theorem~7.3]{frohlichspencer1981transition} recalls the low-temperature argument. The notebook attribution to Dobrushin is broader than the exact SOS proof identified here.',('frohlichspencer1981transition',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='An exponential bound replaces an unsupported asymptotic equivalence; the finite-volume zero-boundary convention is retained.')
add(572,'Logarithmic height-difference fluctuations at small inverse temperature','P001056','theorem',r'For small \(\beta\), \(c_2\log(1+|p-q|)\le\mathbb E(h_p-h_q)^2\le c_1\log(1+|p-q|)\).',r'In a thermodynamic gradient-state limit of the two-dimensional SOS model, for sufficiently small \(\beta>0\) there are positive finite \(c_-(\beta),c_+(\beta)\) bounding \(\mathbb E(h_p-h_q)^2\) above and below by the corresponding multiples of \(\log(1+|p-q|)\). This is a statement about differences; unpinned absolute heights need not have a finite-variance infinite-volume law.',r'The lower bound is \citet[Theorem~7.3 and equation~(7.18)]{frohlichspencer1981transition}; the matching upper bound is recorded in \citet[Theorem~4(2)]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces}. The 1981 publication predates this talk.',('frohlichspencer1981transition','frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces'),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Gradient limits, coupling-dependent constants and the distinction between absolute and relative heights are stated.')
add(572,'Microscopic height shifts versus an enhanced continuous symmetry','P001056','claim',r'The product weight is said to have continuous symmetry \(h_p\mapsto h_p+\mathrm{const}\), broken by the boundary condition, and to gain symmetry on shrinking the squares.',r'For integer-valued heights the exact global shifts are \(h\mapsto h+k\), \(k\in\mathbb Z\); zero boundary values break even this action on a fixed finite-volume state space. Real shifts occur in a continuous-field representation or a scaling description, not as transformations of the original integer configurations. A scaling-limit claim additionally needs an observable topology and rescaling prescription.',r'\citet[Section~2, equations~(28)--(29)]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces} puts the real shift symmetry in a renormalized continuous-field representation.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The discrete symmetry and the formal enhanced symmetry are separated.')
add(572,'The passage from height graphs to general plaquette surfaces','P001056','construction',r'\(Z_{\beta,\gamma}=\sum_{\partial S=\gamma}e^{-\beta|S|}\), and the analogous small-\(\beta\) questions are said not to be done.',r'To define a finite count, specify connected surfaces attached to a fixed finite boundary \(\gamma\), the ambient lattice, permitted intersections, plaquette multiplicities and topology. If disconnected components can be placed arbitrarily far away, even a fixed-area count can be infinite. Connected sets with each plaquette used at most once have at most exponential counting growth, by bounded-degree lattice-animal enumeration.',r'\citet[Section~3, ensembles (a)--(c)]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces} explicitly distinguishes the alternatives. Its SOS theorem does not apply to all of them.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='Connectedness and multiplicity restrictions are necessary editorial qualifications; no unique general ensemble is silently selected.')
add(572,'The asserted exponential times power counting asymptotic','P001056','claim',r'\(a_\gamma(n)=\#\{S:\partial S=\gamma,|S|=n\}\sim C_\gamma^n n^{\delta(\gamma)}\), allegedly from general combinatorial theorems.',r'An exponential growth rate, where established, means \(\log a_\gamma(n)=n\log C+o(n)\) along admissible area values. It does not imply a polynomial prefactor: for example \(a_n=\lfloor C^ne^{\sqrt n}\rfloor\) has the same exponential rate and no form \(A C^n n^\delta\). The notebook does not fix the surface class or area periodicity needed for the stronger claim.',r'\citet[abstract]{durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces} states only exponential growth with an \(o(n)\) remainder for its two specified models. The counterexample to the inference is an elementary check \ours.',('durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The verified leading growth is separated from the unverified power-law refinement.',status='the quoted general-combinatorics justification does not establish the displayed asymptotic; its exact model-dependent version is not determined by the notes.')
add(572,'Boundary independence of the exponential growth rate','P001056','theorem',r'The limit \(n^{-1}\log a_\gamma(n)\) is introduced and \(C_\gamma\) is asserted independent of \(\gamma\).',r'For the self-avoiding and planar random-surface models considered by Durhuus--Fröhlich--Jónsson, the admissible-area growth satisfies \(a_\gamma(n)=\exp(\beta_0n+o(n))\), with \(\beta_0\) independent of the fixed admissible boundary. A finite boundary modification explains the mechanism, but it must preserve the chosen surface class; it is not a theorem about every object called a surface.',r'\citet[abstract]{durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces} is a direct later match, published 31 October 1983 with the speaker as coauthor. Exact preprint date and full proof were not verified.',('durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces',),cl='A',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='exact',reconstruction='The theorem is restricted to the identified models and admissible areas.')
add(572,'Does the subexponential exponent depend only on boundary topology?','P001056','conjecture',r'\(\delta(\gamma)\) should depend on topological properties of \(\gamma\); sketches contrast one and several boundary components.',r'Once a particular asymptotic \(a_\gamma(n)\sim A_\gamma C^n n^{\delta_\gamma}\) and a surface ensemble are fixed, one can ask whether \(\delta_\gamma\) depends only on the number and knot/link type of boundary components, or also on genus restrictions and embedding dimension. The notes do not choose these parameters and do not establish existence of \(\delta_\gamma\).',r'The exponential theorem in \citet{durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces} does not determine a power exponent. Searches of surface-counting and topology literature did not yield a theorem matching the unqualified conjecture.',('durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',confidence='medium',status='a universal topological exponent cannot be assigned without specifying the ensemble and proving that such an exponent exists.')
add(572,'The number of handles of a typical surface','P001057','question',r'What happens when all topologies are allowed, and how many handles does a typical surface have?',r'A typical genus requires a normalized probability measure: for example a fixed-area ensemble, or a grand-canonical ensemble with specified area and genus weights. It also requires a class of actual surfaces, since a branched plaquette complex need not have a manifold genus. None of the limiting regimes or weights is fixed at this question.',r'The genus-weighted alternatives already appear in \citet[Section~3, equation~(35)]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces}. They illustrate the missing specification rather than resolve a unique notebook problem.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',verification='read the result',match='thematic only',status='the question is underdetermined; results for fixed-topology, tethered, branched or triangulated models cannot be substituted indiscriminately.')
add(572,'The partition-function abscissa from counting growth','P001057','theorem',r'\(Z_{\beta,\gamma}<\infty\) for \(\beta>\beta_0\) and \(Z_{\beta,\gamma}=\infty\) for \(\beta<\beta_0\); the equality cases are overwritten.',r'For finite nonnegative counts \(a_\gamma(n)\), set \(\beta_0=\limsup_n n^{-1}\log a_\gamma(n)\). The root test gives convergence of \(\sum_n a_\gamma(n)e^{-\beta n}\) for \(\beta>\beta_0\) and divergence for \(\beta<\beta_0\). At equality both behaviors are possible. This statement remains valid without a polynomial correction or a true growth-rate limit.',r'This elementary power-series fact underlies the surface partition functions in \citet[Section~3, equations~(36)--(37)]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces}. The limsup formulation is supplied directly \ours.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Finite counts and strict inequalities avoid unsupported critical-point claims.')
add(572,'Divergent normalization does not define a positive-density surface phase','P001057','claim',r'When \(Z_{\beta,\gamma}=\infty\), the density of occupied plaquettes is positive, as for a Peano curve.',r'An infinite unnormalized sum does not define probabilities by \(Z^{-1}w\), and therefore by itself implies no density statement. A finite-volume ensemble and thermodynamic limit could define a dense phase, but these are additional choices. Divergence of a one-surface generating function and positive-density plaquette percolation are different statements.',r'The distinct ensembles in \citet[Section~3]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces} do not justify this implication. Its failure is logical: the probability measure used to state the conclusion is absent.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces',),cl='F',literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='none found',status='the asserted implication remains invalid without a specified normalized finite-volume construction.')
add(572,'The conjectured roughening interval for general surfaces','P001057','conjecture',r'For \(\beta_0<\beta<\beta_R\), fluctuations should be logarithmic. For \(\beta>\beta_R\), the notes write roughening but describe a nearly flat shape.',r'The description at large inverse temperature is the smooth or rigid regime. Roughening means loss of this rigidity on decreasing \(\beta\). For general embedded plaquette surfaces, the existence of a distinct transition requires a chosen ensemble and a fluctuation observable; the height difference of the SOS graph is no longer automatically defined.',r'\citet[Section~3, equations~(42)--(45)]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces} formulates model-specific roughening questions. \citet{durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces} discusses, rather than proves, such a transition. The 1981 SOS theorem cannot settle this separate conjecture.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces','durhuusfrohlichjonsson1983surfaces'),cl='F',literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',status='the general-surface question lacks a unique ensemble/observable; the reversed phase terminology is a definite correction.')
add(572,'The zero-color limit as an analytic interpolation','P001057','construction',r'The surface theory is equivalent to \(SU(N)\) gauge theory as \(N\to0\), with Maritan and Omero in Physics Letters cited; the weight \(w_N(S)\) tends to \(w(S)\).',r'This means an analytic interpolation of suitably rescaled lattice-gauge expansions and Wilson observables in the color parameter. In the identified small-coupling construction the zero-color limit yields a self-avoiding surface generating function. It is not a limit through positive integer group ranks approaching zero, and it is not the unrestricted continuum Yang--Mills theory.',r'\citet[Theorem~6(2), reference~28]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces} records the construction and cites the 1981 CERN preprint. The original Maritan--Omero paper was not obtained; verification of its result is indirect through this primary account.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces',),verification='indirect',match='exact',reconstruction='Analytic continuation, field rescaling and the small-coupling restriction are restored.')
add(572,'The proposed edge-length measure and its scale divergence','P001057','construction',r'The weight contains area, vertex angle defects, a denominator \(e^{-c\,\#\mathrm{handles}}\), and \(\prod_{\rm edges}d\ell/\ell\).',r'For a fixed closed triangulation, lengths must be positive and satisfy triangle inequalities. The displayed denominator equals a genus factor \(e^{cg}\), not suppression when \(c>0\). The bare measure is not normalizable over all lengths: write \(\ell_e=r u_e\), fix one shape coordinate, and restrict the other ratios to a compact nondegenerate open set. The product measure includes \(dr/r\), angle defects are scale invariant, and area is \(r^2A(u)\); hence the integral diverges at \(r=0\) for finite couplings. An area constraint, scale quotient or cutoff is essential.',r'The Regge functional-integral setting is discussed in \citet{cheegermullerschrader1982lattice}. The explicit small-scale divergence is an editorial calculation \ours, independent of any continuum conjecture.',('cheegermullerschrader1982lattice',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='Admissible lengths and a fixed closed triangulation specify the counterexample; the notebook itself does not supply a normalization or regulator.')
add(572,'Regge curvature concentrated on codimension-two hinges','P001057','definition',r'\(R=\sum_f\operatorname{area}(f)\,\operatorname{defect}(f)\), with \(f\) a \((d-2)\)-simplex; the reference says Regge 64.',r'In a piecewise Euclidean \(d\)-manifold, an interior hinge \(f\) has deficit \(\epsilon_f=2\pi-\sum_{\sigma\supset f}\theta_{\sigma,f}\). In the convention \(\mathrm{Scal}=2K\) in dimension two, the integrated scalar-curvature measure is \(2\sum_f\operatorname{Vol}_{d-2}(f)\epsilon_f\). A convention absorbing the factor two is possible, but must be stated. Boundary terms require exterior-angle contributions.',r'The foundational reference is \citet{regge1961relativity}, published in February 1961. This recurs from 1981-546.6; the formula is defined carefully in \citet[Section~2]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature}.',('regge1961relativity','cheegermullerschrader1984curvature'),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Deficits, normalization and the boundary qualification are supplied.')
add(572,'Weak convergence of piecewise-flat curvature measures','P001057','theorem',r'The hinge sum tends to \(\int\sqrt g\,R(x)\,d^dx\) as the triangulation approaches a smooth surface or manifold.',r'For geodesic triangulations approximating a smooth compact Riemannian manifold, with mesh tending to zero and uniform positive fatness of simplices, the corresponding piecewise-flat Lipschitz--Killing curvature measures converge weakly to their smooth counterparts. In particular the consistently normalized hinge sum converges to total scalar curvature. Arbitrarily thin simplices or pointwise hinge-curvature convergence are not covered.',r'The precise result is \citet[Theorem~5.1]{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature}. Its 1981 announcement and 1982 chapter precede this talk; the earlier occurrence is 1981-546.7. This differs from convergence of Regge field equations in GP-1981-21.',('cheegermullerschrader1982lattice','cheegermullerschrader1984curvature'),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The approximation, fatness and measure topology are essential hypotheses.')
add(572,'Does the displayed random-metric integral converge to Polyakov theory?','P001057','question',r'After the curvature theorem, the notes claim that equation (14) tends to Polyakov string theory, followed by Exercise: Prove this!',r'Convergence of deterministic curvature actions does not prove convergence of probability measures or functional integrals. The proposed limit lacks a triangulation ensemble, scale normalization, treatment of degenerating lengths, gauge quotient, matter fields, coupling renormalization and a topology of convergence. Even the unregulated fixed-triangulation measure above diverges. These omissions prevent a unique theorem from being reconstructed.',r'Compare the conjectural discussion in \citet[Section~3]{frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces} with the deterministic theorem of \citet{cheegermullerschrader1984curvature} and Polyakov\\textquotesingle s formal integral \citep{polyakov1981quantum}. None proves the claimed measure limit.',('frohlichpfisterspencer1982surfaces','cheegermullerschrader1984curvature','polyakov1981quantum'),cl='F',literal='not determinable',verification='read the result',match='thematic only',status='the notebook formulation does not specify a continuum-limit problem; modern results for other random-surface measures do not settle this displayed unregulated integral.')
corr('P001055','Equation (1), height-domain notation',r'\(p\subset\mathbb Z^2\).',r'\(p\in\mathbb Z^2\), or let \(p\) denote a plaquette indexed by \(\mathbb Z^2\).','Scan P001055 has a subset-shaped sign; the surrounding height map and plaquette explanation show the intended indexing.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001056','Equation (5), tail equivalence',r'\(\Pr(|h_p|=n)\sim e^{-{\rm const}\,n}\).',r'An exponential upper bound is justified by the stated Peierls argument; a sharp asymptotic requires an additional theorem.','Scan P001056 confirms the tilde. Fröhlich--Spencer Section 7 records bounded low-temperature fluctuations, not the exact tail constant asserted here.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001056','Continuous symmetry of integer heights',r'\(h_p\mapsto h_p+\mathrm{const}\) is continuous for the SOS measure.',r'Exact microscopic shifts require an integer constant; the real shift belongs to a continuous-field representation or limit.',r'Scan P001055 fixes \(h_p\in\mathbb Z\); P001056 calls the shift continuous. Noninteger shifts leave the configuration space.')
corr('P001056','Equation (9), combinatorial justification',r'\(a_\gamma(n)\sim C_\gamma^n n^{\delta(\gamma)}\) follows from general combinatorial theorems.',r'The identified 1983 theorem gives \(\log a_\gamma(n)=\beta_0 n+o(n)\), with no general power-law refinement.',r'Scan P001056 confirms the stronger formula. The checked Durhuus--Fröhlich--Jónsson abstract states only exponential growth; \(C^ne^{\sqrt n}\) illustrates the logical gap.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001057','Equations (12)--(13), overwritten endpoints',r'Possibly \(\beta\ge\beta_0\) and \(\beta\le\beta_0\).',r'Use strict \(\beta>\beta_0\) for convergence and \(\beta<\beta_0\) for divergence; leave equality undecided.','Scan P001057 is overwritten. The root test alone says nothing at the critical value.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001057','Density conclusion after equation (13)',r'\(Z_{\beta,\gamma}=\infty\) implies positive occupied-plaquette density.',r'A finite-volume normalized measure and a specified limit are needed before asserting a density.',r'The scan makes this implication explicitly. An infinite normalizing constant does not define the probability law used in the conclusion.')
corr('P001057','Roughening label at large inverse temperature',r'\(\beta>\beta_R\): roughening; almost flat, nearly constant shape.',r'Call the described large-\(\beta\) phase smooth or rigid. Roughening occurs on the smaller-\(\beta\) side.','Scan P001057 confirms the English word and contradictory explanatory prose; the preceding SOS statements have the correct temperature order.')
corr('P001057','Equation (14), handle factor and scale',r'The handle factor is in the denominator, \(e^{-cg}\), with \(\prod_e d\ell_e/\ell_e\).',r'The displayed sign gives \(e^{cg}\). Retain it unless a suppression convention is explicitly intended; specify a scale constraint or regulator before normalization.',r'The fraction bar is clear in scan P001057. Global scaling gives \(dr/r\), unchanged deficits and area \(r^2A\), proving small-scale divergence of the bare closed-triangulation integral.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001057','Regge date and curvature normalization','Regge 64; unqualified equality of hinge sum and scalar-curvature integral.',r'Regge 1961. For the convention \(\mathrm{Scal}=2K\), the hinge sum has an overall factor two; state any alternative normalization.','Scan P001057 shows 64. The opened publisher record gives February 1961. In dimension two the deficit sum is total Gaussian curvature, half total usual scalar curvature.',type='provenance')
corr('P001057','Curvature theorem versus functional-integral limit','Curvature convergence implies that equation (14) tends to Polyakov theory.','Keep the proposed measure limit separate from deterministic weak curvature convergence.','Cheeger--Müller--Schrader Theorem 5.1 assumes fat metric approximations and proves curvature-measure convergence, not convergence of the displayed edge-length probability measure.',type='ambiguity')
audit.append('## Record 572\nActual P001055--P001057, all Russian and scans read. Located exact matching primary lecture Fröhlich--Pfister--Spencer1982 https://www.numdam.org/item/RCP25_1982__30__84_0.pdf, read Sections2--4, Theorem4 and Theorem6(2); IHES/P/82/20 also opened, no exact issue day verified. This source distinguishes SOS, self-avoiding, planar and branched ensembles and gives the analytic-interpolation interpretation of the N-to-zero limit. Fröhlich--Spencer1981 full IHES/P/81/25 https://repo-archives.ihes.fr/FONDS_IHES/I_Prepublications/FROHLICH/1981-2002/P_81_25/P_81_25_web.pdf, Section7 Theorem7.3 and remarks read; publisher DOI10.1007/BF01208273 December1981 metadata. Durhuus--Fröhlich--Jónsson1983 institutional abstract https://iris.hi.is/en/publications/self-avoiding-and-planar-random-surfaces-on-the-lattice/ opened; exact exponential-plus-o(n) scope and boundary independence, full publisher article403. Maritan--Omero CERN original and catalogue hit were blocked by bot challenge; no false full-read reference added, theorem verification indirect through primary1982lecture Theorem6(2). Regge publisher DOI10.1007/BF02733251 opened; fulloriginalnotread; CMS1982chapter DOI10.1007/3-540-11560-9_12 metadata opened; CMS1984full https://www.cs.jhu.edu/~misha/Fall09/Cheeger84.pdf Theorem5.1 and assumptions read. Previous exact occurrences1981-546.6/.7 retained; GP-1981-21 field-equation convergence is distinct. Current primary search on self-avoiding-surface roughening and topology found2026 Chen et al APS DOI10.1103/28pb-jly6, whose opened abstract reports simulations of tethered elastic surfaces, not the notebook ensemble; 2025 Duncan--Schweinhart full publisher DOI10.1007/s00220-025-05322-5 concerns homological plaquette random clusters with coefficient-field dependence, not a universal genus exponent. Neither is substituted as a solution. Own checks: shift integrality, disconnected-component infinite count, root test, subexponential counterexample, genus-factor sign, global-scale divergence and factor-two curvature convention.')
